\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. All Taylor coefficients below are taken at $0$. A family of meromorphic functions on $\mathbb D$ is normal if every sequence has a subsequence converging locally uniformly in the spherical metric to a meromorphic function or to the constant $\infty$. Differential inequalities below mean that the indicated meromorphic functions never take the stated finite value; poles do not violate a finite-value omission.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For each fixed integer $n\ge3$, is the family
\[\mathcal G_n=\{f\text{ meromorphic on }\mathbb D:f'(z)f(z)^n\ne1\text{ for every }z\in\mathbb D\}\]
normal on $\mathbb D$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Historical question: does omission of one by the differential monomial $f'f^n$ ensure normality for meromorphic disc functions when $n$ is at least three? The source update reports an affirmative answer.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 2305013 (AMR-022-5013). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] W. K. Hayman and E. F. Lingham, Research Problems in Function Theory (2018), arXiv:1809.07200, Chapter 5, Problem 5.13, its update and the preceding shared notation. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
